WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 1 separated company-specific risk, which diversification removes and the market does not pay for, from market risk, which it cannot and does. It also built the required-return ladder: a risk-free rate plus a premium. This chapter measures the premium.
2.1  From one security to a portfolio
The expected return of a portfolio is the weighted average of the expected returns of what is in it. Put 60 per cent of the money into a share expected to return 12 per cent and 40 per cent into one expected to return 8 per cent: 0.6 × 12 = 7.2 and 0.4 × 8 = 3.2, so the portfolio expects 7.2 + 3.2 = 10.4 per cent. No subtlety at all.
The risk of a portfolio is not the weighted average of the risks, and that single fact is the foundation of modern finance. It is less, unless the two assets move in perfect step. The reason is that when one is having a bad year the other may be having a good one, and the two partly cancel.
How much they cancel is measured by the correlation coefficient, which runs from minus one to plus one. At plus one the two move in exact proportion and nothing cancels. At zero they are unrelated. At minus one they are mirror images and, in the right proportions, cancel exactly.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the same two shares, three correlations
Step 1. The holdings. Sixty per cent in a share with a standard deviation of 20 points and forty per cent in one with 10 points. The contributions are 0.6 × 20 = 12 and 0.4 × 10 = 4.
Step 2. Perfect positive correlation. Nothing offsets, so the portfolio's standard deviation is the weighted average: 12 + 4 = 16 points.
Step 3. Zero correlation. The squared contributions add instead: 12 squared is 144 and 4 squared is 16, so the variance is 144 + 16 = 160 and the standard deviation is 12.65 points.
Step 4. Perfect negative correlation. The contributions offset directly: 12 − 4 = 8 points.
Check. The expected return is 10.4 per cent in all three cases, because correlation does not touch the return. Only the risk moved, from 16 points to 8, and the investor paid nothing for the improvement. That free improvement is what the word diversification means, and it is the only free thing in finance.
Figure F602-02 sets the three out.
Figure F602-02. One portfolio, three correlations
@table
Correlation | What it means | Portfolio standard deviation | Expected return
Plus 1.0 | The two move in exact step | 16.0 points | 10.4%
Zero | The two are unrelated | 12.65 points | 10.4%
Minus 1.0 | The two are mirror images | 8.0 points | 10.4%
@end
EXAM TRAP   Returns average, risks do not
A portfolio's expected return is always the weighted average. Its standard deviation is the weighted average only at a correlation of exactly plus one, and is lower otherwise.
Correlation of zero does not mean no risk. The variance still adds.
Covariance and correlation measure the same relationship. The correlation coefficient is the covariance scaled to run between minus one and plus one, which is why the exam quotes it.
