2.2  Diversification, and where it stops
Add a third share to the two, then a tenth, then a fiftieth. Each new holding brings its own company-specific risk and partly cancels the others', and the portfolio's standard deviation falls — quickly at first and then more slowly.
It does not fall to zero. What is left when the company-specific risk has gone is the part every share has in common, which is the market itself. A portfolio of fifty shares still falls in a recession, because a recession is not a company-specific event.
The practical numbers are worth remembering. Most of the benefit arrives within the first fifteen or twenty holdings; beyond thirty, adding names changes very little. The risk that remains is the systematic risk of Chapter 1, and it is the floor.
Three things limit diversification in practice, and the exam names them.
Correlation is rarely low within one market. Shares in the same country and the same industry move together, so a portfolio of twenty food manufacturers is far less diversified than a portfolio of twenty companies in twenty industries.
Diversification across borders works until it is needed. International holdings reduce risk in normal conditions, and correlations rise sharply in a crisis, which is when the protection was wanted.
A company diversifying on its own account is not doing its shareholders a favour. A shareholder can buy shares in a glass works directly and more cheaply than Orontes can buy the glass works. That is the standard objection to conglomerate acquisitions, and Book 3 returns to it.
Figure F602-03 draws the fall.
Figure F602-03. What happens as holdings are added
@table
Number of holdings | Total risk | What has gone | What remains
1 | Highest | Nothing | All company risk and all market risk
10 | Much lower | Most company-specific risk | Market risk and some company risk
30 | Near the floor | Almost all company-specific risk | Market risk
100 | At the floor | All company-specific risk | Market risk only
@end
