2.3  Beta
If the only risk that is rewarded is the part that moves with the market, then what has to be measured is how much a given share moves with the market. That measure is beta.
Beta is the sensitivity of a security's return to the return on the market as a whole. The market portfolio has a beta of 1.0 by definition. A share with a beta of 1.5 tends to rise 15 per cent when the market rises 10 and to fall 15 when it falls 10. A share with a beta of 0.5 moves half as far in each direction. A beta below zero is rare and means the share tends to move against the market.
Three properties matter and all three are examined.
Beta measures systematic risk only. A company can have enormous company-specific risk and a low beta, because its risks have nothing to do with the economy. A gold miner is the standard example and a pharmaceutical company in a trial is another.
Betas are weighted-average additive. The beta of a portfolio is the weighted average of the betas in it: half in a share of beta 0.9 and half in one of 1.5 gives 0.5 × 0.9 = 0.45 and 0.5 × 1.5 = 0.75, so the portfolio beta is 0.45 + 0.75 = 1.20.
Beta is driven by the business and by the financing. A company whose sales swing with the economy has a higher beta than one whose sales do not — which is why a food manufacturer like Orontes, selling a staple, has a beta below 1.0 while a car maker has one above. Debt raises it further, because the fixed interest magnifies whatever the business does. That is the degree of financial leverage of Book 1, Chapter 8, seen in the market's measure.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: what a change in beta does to a price
Step 1. The share today. A share pays a dividend of 0.60 next year, growing at 4 per cent a year for ever. Its beta is 0.90, the risk-free rate is 4 per cent and the market is expected to return 10 per cent.
Step 2. The required return. The market premium is 10.0 − 4.0 = 6.0 points, and at a beta of 0.90 the premium earned is 0.90 × 6.0 = 5.4 points, so the required return is 9.4 per cent.
Step 3. The price. Chapter 5 derives the model; here it is enough that the price is the dividend over the required return less the growth rate: 0.60 ÷ 0.054 = 11.11.
Step 4. The beta rises. Suppose the company borrows heavily and its beta rises to 1.20. The premium becomes 1.20 × 6.0 = 7.2 points and the required return 11.2 per cent, so the price becomes 0.60 ÷ 0.072 = 8.33.
Check. Nothing about the dividend changed. The share fell 25 per cent because the market now requires more to hold it, and the only thing that moved was the sensitivity of its returns to the economy. That is the whole of what the outcome means by the impact of a change in beta on a security's price, and it is the reason a chapter on capital structure later in this book is a chapter about shareholders and not only about lenders.
Figure F602-04 reads a range of betas.
Figure F602-04. What a beta says
@table
Beta | Behaviour against the market | Typical business | Required return at rf 4%, premium 6%
1.60 | Moves 60 per cent further | Construction, luxury goods | 13.6%
1.00 | Moves with the market | The market portfolio | 10.0%
0.90 | Moves slightly less | Food manufacturing | 9.4%
0.40 | Moves far less | Regulated utility | 6.4%
@end
