2.4  The Capital Asset Pricing Model
The model states the required return on any security as the risk-free rate plus beta times the market risk premium.
Each of the three inputs is a question in itself. The risk-free rate is a government security of matching maturity. The market risk premium is the extra return the market as a whole is expected to earn over that rate, which is estimated from long runs of history and typically quoted between four and six points. Beta is estimated by regressing the security's returns on the market's, usually over five years of monthly data.
Apply it to Orontes. At a risk-free rate of 4 per cent, a market premium of 6 points and a beta of 0.90, the premium earned is 0.90 × 6.0 = 5.4 points and the required return on Orontes's equity is 9.4 per cent. Book 1 charged its shareholders 10 per cent when it computed economic profit, and said at the time that the figure would be justified later. This is the justification, and the two are close enough that the conclusion there — a positive economic profit of about 1.1 million dollars — survives either number.
Drawn as a line, with beta on one axis and required return on the other, the model is the security market line. Its intercept is the risk-free rate and its slope is the market risk premium. Every correctly priced security sits on the line. A security above it offers more return than its risk requires and is a buy; one below it is a sell — and the act of buying and selling moves prices until it sits on the line again, which is what the model means by equilibrium.
Figure F602-05 works four securities.
Figure F602-05. The security market line at a 4 per cent risk-free rate and a 6-point premium
@table
Security | Beta | Required return | If it is expected to return 10% | Verdict
Treasury bill | 0.00 | 4.0% | n/a | The intercept
Orontes | 0.90 | 9.4% | 10.0% | Above the line: a buy
The market | 1.00 | 10.0% | 10.0% | On the line
A construction firm | 1.60 | 13.6% | 10.0% | Below the line: a sell
@end
EXAM TRAP   Expected against required
The CAPM gives the required return — what the risk deserves. The expected return is what the analyst thinks the security will deliver. The comparison of the two is the investment decision, and a question that gives you both is asking for exactly that comparison.
The market risk premium is the market return less the risk-free rate, not the market return. Multiplying beta by the whole market return is the commonest arithmetic error on this outcome.
A beta of zero gives the risk-free rate, not a zero return.
