Chapter summary
Figure F602-07 brings the chapter together.
Figure F602-07. Chapter 2 summary map: the key rule for each section.
A portfolio's expected return is the weighted average of what is in it, and its risk is not: the same two shares give a standard deviation of 16 points at a correlation of plus one, 12.65 at zero and 8 at minus one, with the expected return unmoved at 10.4 per cent in all three. Adding holdings drives company-specific risk out, quickly to about twenty names and almost completely by thirty, and stops at the floor of market risk — which is why a company that diversifies on its shareholders' behalf is usually doing something they could do more cheaply themselves. What is left is measured by beta, the sensitivity of a security to the market, which is weighted-average additive across a portfolio, rises with the cyclicality of the business and with debt, and moves prices directly: a beta rising from 0.90 to 1.20 takes a required return from 9.4 to 11.2 per cent and the same unchanged dividend stream from 11.11 to 8.33, a fall of a quarter. The Capital Asset Pricing Model states the required return as the risk-free rate plus beta times the market premium, puts Orontes at 9.4 per cent against the 10 Book 1 assumed, and draws every correctly priced security on the security market line. And the model's assumptions are all false, its market portfolio unobservable and its premium a matter of estimate — which is why its structure, not its precision, is what survives.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
Multiple-choice questions
P2-01  Sixty per cent of a portfolio returns 12 per cent and forty per cent returns 8 per cent. The portfolio returns:
A.	10.4 per cent
B.	10.0 per cent
C.	20.0 per cent
D.	9.6 per cent
