A.	the expected market return less the risk-free rate.
B.	the expected market return.
C.	beta times the risk-free rate.
D.	the standard deviation of the market.
P2-10  The weakest practical element of the CAPM is:
A.	that the market portfolio is unobservable and the premium only estimated.
B.	that it uses a risk-free rate.
C.	that returns rise with risk.
D.	that beta is additive across a portfolio.
Case-style practice: the required return on Orontes
Orontes's board is setting the return it will require from new investment. The treasurer proposes using the 6 per cent the company pays Levant Commerce Bank, on the ground that it is the company's actual cost of money. The chief financial officer proposes the CAPM, with a risk-free rate of 4 per cent, a market risk premium of 6 points and an equity beta of 0.90.
Required. Compute the CAPM figure. Say which proposal you would adopt and why, and name two inputs whose estimation you would want to see before relying on the number. Under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC2-1  Answer A. Returns weight exactly.
B is wrong. A portfolio cannot return more than its best holding.
C is wrong. Correlation affects risk, not expected return.
D is wrong. The weights are precisely what the average uses.
SC2-2  Answer A. Only at perfect positive correlation does nothing offset.
B is wrong. At zero correlation the variances add and the standard deviation is lower.
C is wrong. At minus one the holdings offset most strongly.
D is wrong. The relationship holds only at plus one.
SC2-3  Answer A. Mirror-image returns cancel in the right proportions.
B is wrong. Negative correlation reduces risk.
C is wrong. Expected return is unaffected by correlation.
D is wrong. Such pairs are exactly what a hedger looks for.
SC2-4  Answer A. The floor is market risk.
B is wrong. No portfolio of risky assets is riskless.
C is wrong. Risk falls as holdings are added.
D is wrong. The fall is the point of diversifying.
SC2-5  Answer A. Shares in one industry move together.
B is wrong. Size is not what limits the diversification here.
C is wrong. Food is a defensive industry.
D is wrong. Dividends do not determine correlation.
SC2-6  Answer A. A shareholder can buy the other business directly.
B is wrong. Diversification does reduce risk; the question is who should do it.
C is wrong. Acquisitions are lawful and common.
D is wrong. Market risk is not reduced by either route.
SC2-7  Answer A. A beta of 1.5 moves half as far again as the market.
B is wrong. That describes a beta of about 0.67.
C is wrong. Moving against the market requires a negative beta.
D is wrong. A beta of 1.5 is more volatile than the market, not less.
SC2-8  Answer A. Betas are weighted-average additive.
B is wrong. Only the market portfolio has a beta of 1.0 by definition.
C is wrong. The average, not the maximum, is taken.
D is wrong. Diversification removes company risk, not beta.
SC2-9  Answer A. A higher required return discounts the same dividends more heavily.
B is wrong. The required return rose; the dividends did not.
C is wrong. The price moves with the required return.
D is wrong. The price is defined so long as the required return exceeds the growth rate.
SC2-10  Answer A. The premium earned is 1.2 × 6.0 = 7.2 points on top of 4 per cent.
B is wrong. This multiplies beta by the premium and forgets the risk-free rate.
C is wrong. This is the premium alone.
D is wrong. This multiplies beta by the whole market return and adds the risk-free rate.
SC2-11  Answer A. More return than the risk requires means the price is too low.
B is wrong. That describes a security below the line.
C is wrong. A correctly priced security sits on the line.
D is wrong. Nothing here makes the security riskless.
SC2-12  Answer D. The model says nothing about dividends.
A is wrong. Free borrowing and lending at the risk-free rate is an assumption.
B is wrong. Homogeneous expectations is an assumption.
C is wrong. Stable, well-measured betas are assumed in practice.
SC2-13  Answer A. It is an estimate with a wide reputable range.
B is wrong. No exchange publishes it.
C is wrong. Six points is a common assumption, not a fact.
D is wrong. The premium is the excess over the risk-free rate.
SC2-14  Answer A. The arbitrage pricing theory allows several systematic factors.
B is wrong. It retains a risk-free rate.
C is wrong. No model rewards diversifiable risk.
D is wrong. It rests on diversification as the CAPM does.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Market risk premium | 6.0 points: 9.0 − 3.0 = 6.0.
Barada Wholesale | Required 10.8 per cent, from 1.30 × 6.0 = 7.8 points on top of 3 per cent. Expected 12.0, so it is above the line and a buy.
Orontes Foods | Required 8.4 per cent, from 0.90 × 6.0 = 5.4 points on top of 3 per cent. Expected 8.0, so it is marginally below the line and a sell on these figures.
A utility | Required 6.0 per cent, from 0.50 × 6.0 = 3.0 points on top of 3 per cent. Expected 7.0, so it is above the line and a buy.
The portfolio | Beta 0.70, from 0.5 × 0.90 = 0.45 and 0.5 × 0.50 = 0.25, so 0.45 + 0.25 = 0.70; the required return is 7.2 per cent, from 0.70 × 6.0 = 4.2 points on top of 3 per cent.
What it tells you | Two of the three are mispriced on these estimates, and the one that looks dearest — the utility, on the lowest expected return — is the best buy of the three, because its risk is so low that 7.0 per cent is more than it needs to offer. Ranking by expected return alone reverses the answer.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P2-01  Answer A. 0.6 × 12 = 7.2 and 0.4 × 8 = 3.2, so 7.2 + 3.2 = 10.4 per cent.
B is wrong. This is the simple average, which ignores the weights.
C is wrong. The two returns do not add.
D is wrong. This reverses the weights.
P2-02  Answer A. Perfect negative correlation gives the largest offset.
B is wrong. Zero correlation reduces risk, but less.
C is wrong. Plus one removes the benefit entirely.
D is wrong. Correlation cannot exceed plus one.
P2-03  Answer A. Company-specific risk cancels; market risk does not.
B is wrong. Market risk is what remains.
C is wrong. No portfolio of risky assets is riskless.
D is wrong. Diversification removes a great deal.
P2-04  Answer A. Beta is the sensitivity to the market.
B is wrong. Total risk is measured by the standard deviation.
C is wrong. Unsystematic risk is what beta ignores.
D is wrong. Beta is a sensitivity, not a dispersion.
P2-05  Answer A. 0.5 × 0.9 = 0.45 and 0.5 × 1.5 = 0.75, so 0.45 + 0.75 = 1.20.
B is wrong. The betas are weighted, not added.
C is wrong. The average of 0.9 and 1.5 is not 1.0.
D is wrong. This halves the answer twice.
P2-06  Answer A. 0.9 × 6.0 = 5.4 points on top of 4 per cent.
B is wrong. This multiplies beta by the whole market return and adds the risk-free rate.
C is wrong. That is the market return less a point for no reason.
D is wrong. That is the premium alone.
P2-07  Answer A. A higher beta raises the required return and lowers the price.
B is wrong. Higher required returns lower prices.
C is wrong. The discount rate changed, so the price must.
D is wrong. There is no two-stage effect here.
P2-08  Answer A. Above the line means more return than the risk requires.
B is wrong. Overpriced securities plot below the line.
C is wrong. Only a zero-beta asset is riskless in this model.
D is wrong. Equilibrium is on the line.
P2-09  Answer A. The premium is the excess of the market over the risk-free rate.
B is wrong. That omits the subtraction.
C is wrong. Beta multiplies the premium, not the risk-free rate.
D is wrong. The premium is a return, not a dispersion.
P2-10  Answer A. Neither the market portfolio nor the premium can be observed.
B is wrong. A risk-free rate is readily available.
C is wrong. That is the model's strength.
D is wrong. Additivity is a convenience, not a weakness.
Case-style set: the required return on Orontes
The CAPM figure. The market premium is 10.0 − 4.0 = 6.0 points and the beta is 0.90, so the premium earned is 0.90 × 6.0 = 5.4 points and the required return on equity is 9.4 per cent.
Which proposal. The CAPM, and the treasurer's proposal is wrong in a way worth stating precisely. The 6 per cent paid to Levant Commerce Bank is the cost of one source of money, and the cheapest one, because the bank is paid before the shareholders and holds security. Using it as the hurdle for new investment would approve projects that earn 7 per cent while the shareholders who stand behind the bank require more than 9 — which destroys value while appearing to create it. The correct hurdle is the weighted average of what every provider of capital requires, and Chapter 8 builds it; the 9.4 per cent computed here is the equity component of that average.
Two inputs I would want to see estimated. First, the beta. At 0.90 it is doing a great deal of work, and I would want to know the period it was measured over, whether it reflects Orontes's capital structure as it is now after the 2025 share issue, and whether a listed peer group was used in place of the company's own history.
Second, the market risk premium. Reputable estimates run from about three points to about seven, and that range alone moves the required return from 6.7 to 10.3 per cent — a spread wide enough to change which projects are approved. I would want the source, the period and the method stated, and the final decision tested at both ends of the range.
